Searches covered the six specified gaps. New evidence materially strengthens: the Gaibandha investigation and sanction recommendations; direct BTRC attribution for one rally shutdown; final-phase local-election contestability; and group-specific treatment of Indigenous, LGBTQI+, and Rohingya communities. Saturation searches did not recover a direct 2022 Hasina statement on caretaker arrangements, proof that Gaibandha’s recommended departmental sanctions were completed, or an independent finding that Hasina accepted a judicial, audit, ACC, or NHRC constraint. Transport stoppages remain attributable to owners’ associations, with government direction alleged but unproven.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Gaibandha by-election: Irregularities well-orchestrated","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-12-02","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/politics/news/gaibandha-election-irregularities-well-orchestrated-3184956","claim":"The Election Commission's investigation found coordinated, well-planned irregularities at 125 of 145 Gaibandha-5 polling centers and recommended departmental action against 134 election, administrative, and police officials. It found no evidence implicating the district commissioner, superintendent of police, or any candidate. The EC said it could infer whose orders lay behind the misconduct but could not proceed against planners because witnesses did not name them. This is a substantive institutional finding and sanction recommendation, but not evidence that sanctions were completed or that Hasina accepted or implemented them.","locator":"Lines 164-185 (findings and recommended actions), 187-194 (candidate and senior-official findings), and 196-213 (investigation evidence, coordinated plan, and inability to identify planners)","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C2-001","canonical_fact_key":"bgd_2022_gaibandha_ec_investigation_and_sanction_recommendations","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.4","4B.8"],"source_type":"independent_reporting_of_official_investigation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Named reporter provides detailed, contemporaneous quotations and findings from the EC review and CEC press conference; the underlying report itself was not accessible.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct December 2022 follow-through on an October 2022 election.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional conduct by the Election Commission during Hasina's tenure; no evidence of personal direction, acceptance, obstruction, or implementation by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"bgd_2022_gaibandha_ec_constraint","lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.4","4B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"BNP rally: BTRC orders suspension of 3G-4G services in Rajshahi","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-12-03","url":"https://online.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/politics/news/bnp-rally-btrc-orders-suspension-3g-4g-services-rajshahi-3186091","claim":"The Daily Star reported that mobile-operator sources confirmed a BTRC order suspending 3G and 4G service in Rajshahi from 9:30 a.m. to 8:00 p.m. on the day of the BNP divisional rally; rally participants independently reported loss of service. This supports state-regulator attribution for this shutdown, but does not establish that Hasina personally ordered it.","locator":"Headline and opening five paragraphs: BTRC order, specified 9:30 a.m.-8:00 p.m. period, operator-source confirmation, and participant observations","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C2-002","canonical_fact_key":"bgd_2022_rajshahi_bnp_rally_btrc_mobile_shutdown","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.4","4B.5","4B.7","4B.9"],"source_type":"independent_contemporaneous_reporting","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting attributes the order to operator sources and supplies exact timing, but neither the BTRC order nor a named operator was published.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns the 3 December 2022 BNP rally.","ruler_attribution":"Implemented by the state telecommunications regulator under Hasina's government; personal authorization by Hasina is not established.","contrary_evidence":false,"underlying_fact_key":"bgd_2022_rajshahi_bnp_rally_btrc_mobile_shutdown","lenses":["4B.4","4B.5","4B.7","4B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"AL never obstructs BNP rally: Quader","publisher":"Jago News / Bangladesh Sangbad Sangstha","publication_date":"2022-10-20","url":"https://www.jagonews24.com/en/politics/news/64470","claim":"Awami League General Secretary and road transport minister Obaidul Quader denied that either the government or AL obstructed BNP rallies. Addressing the Khulna transport stoppage, he attributed it to transport owners and workers and denied government or party interference. This is the governing party's contemporaneous contrary account, not an independent resolution of who caused the stoppage.","locator":"Paragraphs beginning 'Quader said' through the passage beginning 'About BNP’s allegation,' including the express denials and attribution to transport associations","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C2-003","canonical_fact_key":"bgd_2022_bnp_rally_transport_stoppage_government_denial","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.4","4B.5","4B.9"],"source_type":"official_party_statement","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Reliable for what a senior minister and governing-party official publicly asserted, but not independent evidence that the denial was true.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct statement during the October 2022 BNP rally series.","ruler_attribution":"Statement by Hasina's road transport minister and party general secretary; it represents the governing party position but is not Hasina's own statement.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"bgd_2022_bnp_rally_transport_stoppage_attribution","lenses":["4B.4","4B.5","4B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"7th phase of UP polls: Independent candidates win 64% seats","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-02-08","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/elections/news/7th-phase-polls-independent-candidates-win-64-seats-2957411","claim":"Election Commission data for the seventh Union Parishad phase showed independent candidates winning 86 of 134 declared chairmanships, about 64 percent, while Awami League nominees won 40, about 30 percent. This is concrete contrary evidence that non-AL candidates could defeat ruling-party nominees in the final phase, although many independents may have been AL rebels and the result does not establish nationwide systemic fairness.","locator":"Headline and opening results paragraphs reporting unofficial EC results for 134 union parishads and the 86 independent and 40 AL winners","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C2-004","canonical_fact_key":"bgd_2022_union_parishad_phase7_competitive_results","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.6","4B.10"],"source_type":"independent_reporting_of_official_election_data","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous report gives disaggregated EC result totals; interpretation is limited because independent candidates' affiliations are not fully classified.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct evidence from the 7 February 2022 final Union Parishad phase.","ruler_attribution":"National electoral context under Hasina's government; no personal role in individual contests is established.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"bgd_2022_union_parishad_phase7_competitive_results","lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.6","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Seventh phase of UP polls sees poor turnout for Awami League","publisher":"Dhaka Tribune","publication_date":"2022-02-07","url":"https://www.dhakatribune.com/bangladesh/election/263474/seventh-phase-of-up-polls-sees-poor-turnout-for","claim":"Voting in 138 unions in the seventh and final Union Parishad phase included several violent incidents, and election officials suspended voting in four unions. This qualifies the phase's competitive result evidence by showing that remedies were used in some affected contests but violence and disruption persisted.","locator":"Opening paragraphs stating that 138 unions voted, describing violence, and reporting election officials' suspension of voting in four unions","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C2-005","canonical_fact_key":"bgd_2022_union_parishad_phase7_violence_and_suspensions","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.8","4B.10"],"source_type":"independent_contemporaneous_reporting","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous national reporting identifies concrete election suspensions, but the accessible account does not provide a complete incident-level or complaint/remedy dataset.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct evidence from the 7 February 2022 final Union Parishad phase.","ruler_attribution":"Election-administration conduct and shared local political violence during Hasina's tenure; no personal Hasina direction is shown.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"bgd_2022_union_parishad_phase7_violence_and_suspensions","lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.8","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089131.html","claim":"For LGBTQI+ persons in 2022, the government generally did not actively enforce the penal provision criminalizing same-sex conduct, and official documents included an 'X' or 'Hijra' option while the census included a third-gender category. At the same time, police harassment was reported, investigations of anti-LGBTQI+ violence were rare, formal LGBTQI+ organizations faced registration barriers, and the government took few measures against abuse or coercive conversion practices. The limited recognition evidence should therefore not be treated as equal political participation or an effective remedy.","locator":"Section 6, 'Acts of Violence, Criminalization, and Other Abuses Based on Sexual Orientation…,' lines 412-426; especially 414-418, 422, and 424-425","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C2-006","canonical_fact_key":"bgd_2022_lgbtqi_participation_protection_and_recognition","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.8"],"source_type":"government_human_rights_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed target-year assessment distinguishes law, enforcement, reported police conduct, recognition measures, organization barriers, and remedy failures; some facts rely on civil-society reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Report specifically assesses calendar year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Government laws, documentation systems, registration practices, police conduct, and remedial omissions under Hasina's executive; no personal direction is established.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"bgd_2022_lgbtqi_participation_protection_and_recognition","lenses":["4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089131.html","claim":"Indigenous CHT residents had civil-service and higher-education participation quotas, but the CHT Peace Accord's local-governance provisions remained incompletely implemented and Indigenous people could not participate effectively in decisions affecting their land. In July 2022 the government ordered media not to use 'indigenous' for International Day programming; activists also challenged the census count, while allegations of arbitrary detention, violence, land loss, and unresolved sexual assaults persisted.","locator":"Section 6, 'Indigenous Peoples,' lines 360-376; especially 361-365 for participation and the terminology order, and 368-376 for violence, land disputes, and unresolved remedies","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C2-007","canonical_fact_key":"bgd_2022_indigenous_political_participation_and_remedies","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.8"],"source_type":"government_human_rights_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Target-year assessment provides specific institutional provisions, a dated government order, and carefully attributed allegations; contested claims remain labeled as such.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Specific 2022 conduct is combined with clearly identified continuing institutional conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Central-government policy and implementation omissions under Hasina; the source does not establish her personal direction of particular abuses.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"bgd_2022_indigenous_political_participation_and_remedies","lenses":["4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089131.html","claim":"Rohingya refugees lacked citizenship and full political membership and faced government movement, education, and livelihood restrictions. Hasina personally told the visiting UN human-rights chief that increased Rohingya employment and education opportunities were not possible in Cox's Bazar. Contrary evidence includes government cooperation with UNHCR, the August 2022 endorsement of a skills-development framework, expansion of Myanmar-curriculum education after earlier opposition, health access, vaccination, and approval of an initial 62-person U.S. resettlement group.","locator":"Section 2.e, lines 223-260; especially 227-234 (status and protection), 242-251 (services, restrictions, skills framework, and Hasina statement), and 255-260 (education, health, vaccination, and resettlement)","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C2-008","canonical_fact_key":"bgd_2022_rohingya_membership_restrictions_and_limited_remedies","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.8"],"source_type":"government_human_rights_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed target-year assessment identifies executive responsibilities, a statement attributed directly to Hasina, and specific adverse and favorable implementation measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policies, decisions, program changes, and statement.","ruler_attribution":"Direct for Hasina's reported rejection of expanded Cox's Bazar employment and education; government-level attribution for restrictions and limited corrective programs.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"bgd_2022_rohingya_membership_restrictions_and_limited_remedies","lenses":["4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"World Report 2023: Bangladesh","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/bangladesh","claim":"In 2022 Indigenous activist Nabayan Chakma Milon reportedly died after torture in custody amid broader allegations of security-force abuse, land grabbing, and little redress in the Chittagong Hill Tracts. For LGBTQI+ persons, same-sex conduct remained criminalized and people and advocates faced violence and threats without adequate police protection. These are group-specific protection and remedy failures, not findings of Hasina's personal direction.","locator":"Lines 79-83, especially line 83 for Nabayan Chakma Milon and CHT abuses; lines 94-96 for sexual-orientation and gender-identity conditions","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C2-009","canonical_fact_key":"bgd_2022_indigenous_lgbtqi_violence_and_remedy_failures","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.8"],"source_type":"independent_human_rights_assessment","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Strong independent synthesis with linked underlying reporting, but several abuse components remain allegations and the annual summary provides limited case-level procedural detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The report reviews conduct during calendar year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Security-force and government protection failures during Hasina's administration; no personal direction by Hasina is established.","contrary_evidence":false,"underlying_fact_key":"bgd_2022_indigenous_lgbtqi_violence_and_remedy_failures","lenses":["4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.8"]}
